{
  "abstract": "The regionally proximal relation and the equicontinuous structure relation are fundamental relations in topological dynamics that capture equicontinuous behaviour in a topological dynamical system and its factors. For minimal actions of abelian groups they are known to be equivalence relations and to coincide. This development formalizes in Lean 4 the results of the paper \"The regionally proximal relation for commutative semigroup actions\", which generalizes these facts from group actions to semigroup actions.\nThroughout, a system is an action of a discrete semigroup S on a compact Hausdorff space X by continuous maps. A factor is a continuous S-equivariant surjection. The system X is minimal if every orbit is dense, and equicontinuous if the family of maps {s : X -> X} is uniformly equicontinuous. The regionally proximal relation RP consists of the pairs (x,y) such that for all neighbourhoods U of x and V of y and all neighbourhoods a of the diagonal, there are x' in U, y' in V and s in S with (sx', sy') in a. The equicontinuous structure relation Seq consists of the pairs identified by every equicontinuous factor.\nThe regionally proximal relation has classically been defined only for group actions. The definition used here, for actions of a general semigroup, is the one adopted by Auslander and Dai and by Dai and Xiao; it agrees with the classical notion when the acting semigroup is a group. A second, inequivalent generalization is also natural: the backward regionally proximal relation RP^-, obtained by following the group-action definition of de Vries. For semigroup actions the two differ in general, already for S = (N,+), which is what makes Theorem C below a statement with content.\nTheorem A states that for a commutative semigroup S and a minimal S-system X, a factor is equicontinuous exactly when RP is contained in the relation it induces. Theorem B states that RP and Seq coincide; in particular RP is a closed, S-invariant equivalence relation and X -> X/RP is the largest equicontinuous factor of X. Theorem B is apparently new even for systems generated by a single non-invertible map. Theorem C states that RP coincides with the backward regionally proximal relation RP^-, the second natural generalization of regional proximality from groups to semigroups; the two differ in general for semigroup actions, already for S = (N,+). Theorem D states that commuting minimal actions of two commutative semigroups on the same space induce the same regionally proximal relation.\nTheorems E and F concern natural extensions. Theorem E constructs, for any minimal action of a commutative semigroup, a smallest extension on which the semigroup acts by homeomorphisms: a minimal S-system W and a factor map W -> X through which every extension of X by homeomorphisms factors. The construction goes through the Grothendieck group Gr(S) of S and requires no cancellativity assumption. Theorem F identifies the largest equicontinuous factor of X with that of its natural extension W, both as an S-system and as a Gr(S)-system.\nThe proof of Theorem B follows the cube-space methods of Glasner, Gutman and Ye, generalized in places and specialized in others, using ultrafilters in place of enveloping semigroups and nets to simplify formalization. The development runs to roughly 20,000 lines of Lean across 17 files and formalizes every lemma, proposition, corollary and theorem of the paper; the registered statements are Theorems A through F. The same repository also formalizes a companion paper on the local structure of Delta* sets, registered separately as Palomar entry PALOMAR-2026-10-18-000015.",
